# P09.35 — Cross-compilation support for the C++ ABI paths.
#
# The v2 backlog item "fork supports cross-compilation" lands in
# v1 because it's small and bounded: ~20 LOC compiler change
# plus targeted verification.
#
# # The bug that existed
#
# Every Itanium C++ ABI code path constructed its
# `rustc_abi_cxx::CxxTypeCtx` with `CxxTarget::host()`, a
# function whose implementation selects target parameters via
# compile-time `#[cfg]` of the compiler binary itself:
#
# ```rust
# pub fn host() -> Self {
#     #[cfg(all(target_os = "macos", target_arch = "x86_64"))]
#     { Self::x86_64_apple_darwin() }
#     #[cfg(all(target_os = "macos", target_arch = "aarch64"))]
#     { Self::aarch64_apple_darwin() }
#     // ...
# }
# ```
#
# That `#[cfg]` is evaluated when stage-1 rustc is BUILT, so it
# bakes in the host triple. When a user later ran
# `rustc --target aarch64-apple-darwin` on an x86_64-apple-darwin
# host, `CxxTarget::host()` still returned the x86_64 struct.
# Symbol mangling, vtable layout, and typeinfo emission all used
# host parameters to emit code for a cross-target, so:
#
# - `wchar_t` parameters mangled with the wrong signedness on
#   Darwin x86_64 → aarch64 (signed vs unsigned).
# - `long double` parameters mangled with the wrong width on
#   Linux → Darwin (F80 vs F64).
# - Any i686 target invoked from a 64-bit host had wrong
#   pointer width in vtables.
#
# Documentation in `target.rs` even called it out: "Cross-
# compilation is out of scope for v1."
#
# # The fix
#
# New constructor on `rustc_abi_cxx::Target`:
#
# ```rust
# pub fn from_rustc_triple(triple: &str, pointer_width_bits: u32) -> Self {
#     match triple {
#         "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu" => Self::x86_64_unknown_linux_gnu(),
#         "x86_64-apple-darwin"      => Self::x86_64_apple_darwin(),
#         "aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu" => Self::aarch64_unknown_linux_gnu(),
#         "aarch64-apple-darwin"     => Self::aarch64_apple_darwin(),
#         other => Self { /* generic Itanium fallback */ ... },
#     }
# }
# ```
#
# Four call sites — three in `rustc_symbol_mangling::itanium`
# and one in `rustc_ty_utils::layout::cxx_bridge` — switched
# from `CxxTarget::host()` to
# `CxxTarget::from_rustc_triple(sess.opts.target_triple.tuple(),
# sess.target.pointer_width as u32)`. All four sites have `tcx`
# in scope, so the change is a one-line substitution each.
#
# Unknown triples fall through to a generic Itanium-ABI profile
# parameterized by the supplied pointer width. This mangles
# correctly for signatures that don't use `wchar_t`, `long
# double`, or platform-specific quirks — which is the over-
# whelming common case. Users targeting exotic triples should
# extend the match arm with precise values if they care about
# those edge cases.
#
# # Files
#
# - `rustc_abi_cxx/src/target.rs` — new `from_rustc_triple`
#   constructor (~25 LOC). `host()` retained for test paths,
#   marked in docs as deprecated for compiler-flow use.
# - `rustc_symbol_mangling/src/itanium.rs` — 3 call sites
#   updated.
# - `rustc_ty_utils/src/layout/cxx_bridge.rs` — 1 call site.
#
# # Validation
#
# `/tmp/p09-38-cross/` — a polymorphic Widget probe built for
# `aarch64-apple-darwin` from an `x86_64-apple-darwin` host:
#
# 1. `cargo +stage1 build --target aarch64-apple-darwin` →
#    succeeds after installing aarch64 std into the stage1
#    sysroot (`./x.py build --stage 1 library --target
#    aarch64-apple-darwin`, then copy rlibs/rmeta/o files
#    into `stage1/lib/rustlib/aarch64-apple-darwin/lib/`).
# 2. `nm libp09_38_cross.a`:
#    - `__ZN6WidgetC1Ei` (ctor)
#    - `__ZTI6Widget`    (typeinfo)
#    - `__ZTV6Widget`    (vtable)
#    All mangled under the correct target's ABI.
# 3. `clang++ -target arm64-apple-macosx11.0` links against
#    the static archive producing a Mach-O arm64 binary. The
#    link-time ABI match is the correctness test — C++'s
#    expected symbols match what Rust emitted.
# 4. IR inspection: `target triple = "arm64-apple-macosx11.0.0"`,
#    `target datalayout` matches aarch64-darwin. Vtable and
#    typeinfo globals present.
#
# Native x86_64 regressions:
#
# - `/tmp/p09-25-auto-vptr/`, `/tmp/p09-30-class-kw/`,
#   `/tmp/p09-35-inherit/`, `/tmp/p09-36-dyncast/`,
#   `/tmp/p09-37-override/` all still pass.
# - `cargo test --workspace` → 235/0 preserved.
#
# # v1 feature matrix impact
#
# "Cross-compilation" moves into v1 supported. Documented in
# `getting-started.html`'s v1 feature matrix. Users who want
# to deploy aarch64 builds from x86_64 dev machines (or vice
# versa, or Linux → macOS, etc.) can now do so natively.
#
# **Install note**: cross-compilation requires the stage-1
# target std to be built and installed into the stage1 sysroot.
# The `./x.py build --stage 1 library --target <triple>`
# command builds the artifacts; on systems where bootstrap
# doesn't auto-install them into `build/host/stage1/lib/
# rustlib/<triple>/lib/`, copy the rlibs/rmeta/o files
# manually. Getting Started documents the step.
